      "id": 1,
      "text": "Milwaukee, North Carolina, began as Bethany in 1889, then a 1915 town renamed by railroad conductor Hesikiah Lasker after Wisconsin’s Milwaukee.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milwaukee_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Milwaukee sits on the fall line where North Carolina’s Coastal Plain meets the Piedmont, with the Roanoke River shaping local farming and transport.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milwaukee_north_carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1925 post office sign, crafted by Panton, reads from every angle and still stands as Milwaukee’s quirky landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milwaukee_north_carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Milwaukee’s economy centers on peanut and cotton farming, with old seed‑processing sites and Sidney’s 1948 market still serving the rural community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milwaukee_north_carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Milwaukee’s first school opened in 1876, making it one of the earliest educational institutions in Northampton County.",
